WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:08.275
A card processor keeps its ledger on a leader and two followers, f1 and f2.

2
00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:13.330
Every payment goes to the leader, which adds it to its log at the next position.

3
00:00:13.330 --> 00:00:15.745
Each follower replays that log in order.

4
00:00:15.967 --> 00:00:20.592
f1 is 20 milliseconds away, and f2 is 50.

5
00:00:20.592 --> 00:00:27.585
At one second, the leader is at 250, f1 at 246, and f2 at 238.

6
00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:30.401
The question is when the leader says OK.

7
00:00:30.401 --> 00:00:35.472
The fastest choice is asynchronous: as soon as the payment is on its own disk.

8
00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:37.532
Now the leader dies.

9
00:00:37.532 --> 00:00:44.033
Entries 247 to 250 were still on their way to f1, and they die with it.

10
00:00:44.267 --> 00:00:46.153
Their clients already heard OK.

11
00:00:46.153 --> 00:00:50.168
Four acknowledged payments are on no machine that's still running.

12
00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:52.820
After a timeout, f1 takes over.

13
00:00:52.820 --> 00:00:56.801
It has the highest position, so it loses the least.

14
00:00:56.801 --> 00:00:58.207
f2 now follows f1.

15
00:00:58.433 --> 00:01:00.639
Those four payments never come back.

16
00:01:00.639 --> 00:01:03.396
That's the price of asynchronous replication.

17
00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:07.126
Rewind, and make the leader wait for both followers.

18
00:01:07.126 --> 00:01:09.634
Now nothing acknowledged can be lost.

19
00:01:09.634 --> 00:01:14.991
But every commit waits 100 milliseconds, and if f2 pauses, every payment waits.

20
00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:18.528
Most systems wait for just one follower, here f1.

21
00:01:18.528 --> 00:01:20.973
That's semi-synchronous replication.

22
00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:25.160
f1 confirms each payment 40 milliseconds later.

23
00:01:25.160 --> 00:01:30.131
So clients have heard OK up to 241, and f1 has all of them.

24
00:01:30.333 --> 00:01:32.217
Crash the leader again.

25
00:01:32.217 --> 00:01:37.295
Payments 247 to 250 die with it, but nobody heard OK for them.

26
00:01:37.295 --> 00:01:41.063
f1 takes over with every acknowledged payment.

27
00:01:41.267 --> 00:01:43.941
So the choice is when the leader says OK.

28
00:01:43.941 --> 00:01:47.725
Before a follower has the payment, a failover can lose it.

29
00:01:47.725 --> 00:01:49.095
After that, it can't.
